<h1>Endpoints Management</h1>
<p>An <strong>endpoint</strong> is a machine registered in the controller, running the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/setup/endpoints/">endpoint application</a>, on which steps can run. The
	<strong>Endpoints</strong> panel at the bottom left of the <strong>Execution</strong> tab holds one card per
	registered machine, and every test that names an endpoint field picks its machines from those cards. This page
	covers the panel itself; the pages that follow use the endpoints from tests.</p>
<p>The <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/endpoints/">setup guide</a> shows how to install and run the endpoint
	application and how to add the first endpoint. Here the panel holds the local machine and four others: three
	added by host name, one Windows, one Linux and one macOS machine, and one added by its IP address:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-management/endpoints-frame.png", "Endpoints panel with five cards: localhost, GMG-TEST-1 on Windows 11, GMG-TEST-3 on Linux, GMG-TEST-5 on macOS and 10.100.102.91 on Windows 11, all Secure and Ready, with Load, Save and Manage buttons in the header and an Add button after the cards", "large") }}

<h2 id="endpoint-cards">Endpoint cards</h2>
<p>Each card reads from top to bottom: the name it was added by, in a chip, then the other half of its identity,
	the IP address for a machine added by host name or the host name for a machine added by IP. Below that come the
	operating system with its icon, the version of the endpoint application, <strong>Secure</strong> when the
	connection uses the controller's security material, and the status button.</p>
<p>The <strong>localhost</strong> card is the controller's own machine. It is always present, it cannot be removed,
	and it is where every step runs when a test names no endpoint at all.</p>
<p>The status is the controller's live view of the machine. <strong class="text-success">Ready</strong> means the
	endpoint application answers and can take work. <strong class="text-warning">Online</strong> means the machine
	answers a ping but the application does not, and <strong>Offline</strong> means the machine does not answer at
	all; the setup guide lists the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/setup/endpoints/#troubleshooting">usual causes</a> of both. While a run holds an
	endpoint the card reads <strong>Busy</strong>, and other runs that need the same machine wait for it.</p>

<h2 id="card-actions">Card actions</h2>
<p>Right-click a card to open its actions:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-management/endpoint-card-menu.png", "Context menu of an endpoint card offering Info, Edit, Update, Refresh and Remove", "small") }}
<ul>
	<li><strong>Info</strong> asks the machine for its details and shows them in a dialog.</li>
	<li><strong>Edit</strong> reopens the card in the Add Endpoint dialog to change its type, address, port or
		security.</li>
	<li><strong>Update</strong> installs the controller's own version of the endpoint application on the machine
		and restarts it there, which is how a fleet is kept at one version after a controller upgrade.</li>
	<li><strong>Refresh</strong> checks the status again at once instead of waiting for the next poll.</li>
	<li><strong>Remove</strong> deletes the card after a confirmation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Click <strong>Info</strong> on a card:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-management/endpoint-info-dialog.png", "Endpoint Info dialog for GMG-TEST-1 listing its type, host name, IP, port, Orcraft version, security and status, followed by the operating system, architecture, processor, Python version, CPU count, disk and memory figures", "large") }}
<p>The first block is what the controller knows about the card. The second is what the machine reported when
	asked: operating system and version, architecture, processor, the Python running the endpoint application, and
	its CPU, disk and memory. It is the quickest way to confirm that a machine is the one you think it is.</p>

<h2 id="the-manage-dialog">The Manage dialog</h2>
<p>Click <strong>Manage</strong> in the panel's header to work on every endpoint at once:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-management/manage-endpoints-dialog.png", "Manage Endpoints dialog listing the four remote endpoints in a table with editable name, type, IP and port cells, a Secure check, OS, version, status and per-row refresh and remove buttons, and Select All, Add Endpoint, Apply Changes, Update Selected and Remove Selected buttons below", "large") }}
<p>Each remote endpoint is a row. The name, type, IP and port cells can be edited in place, and
	<strong class="text-accent">Apply Changes</strong> commits every edit together. The handle at the left of a
	row drags it to another position, which is the order the cards and the endpoint fields list the machines in.
	The buttons at the right of a row are the card's <strong>Refresh</strong> and <strong>Remove</strong>, and the
	icons beside the version and the status open <strong>Update</strong> and <strong>Info</strong> for that row.</p>
<p>The check boxes select rows for the two bulk actions. <strong>Update Selected</strong> installs the
	controller's version, shown on the button, on every selected machine, and <strong class="text-danger">Remove
	Selected</strong> deletes them. <strong>Select All</strong> ticks every row, and <strong>Add Endpoint</strong>
	opens the same dialog as the panel's <strong>Add</strong> button.</p>

<h2 id="saving-and-loading-endpoints">Saving and loading endpoints</h2>
<p><strong>Save</strong> in the panel's header writes the registered endpoints to a file, and
	<strong>Load</strong> reads such a file back. The file records each endpoint's type, host name, IP address,
	port and security setting, together with the operating system and version last seen. A team can keep one file
	for its lab and load it on every controller, and it is also the list of machines an
	<a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/endpoints/">AI agent</a> is given when it runs tests.</p>

<h2 id="collapsing-the-panel">Collapsing the panel</h2>
<p>Click the arrow before the <strong>Endpoints</strong> title to fold the panel into a single line:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-management/endpoints-frame-collapsed.png", "Collapsed Endpoints panel showing one green chip per endpoint with its name and operating system icon", "large") }}
<p>Each chip keeps the endpoint's name, its operating system icon and its status colour, so the state of the lab
	stays visible while the execution tree takes the room. Click the arrow again to expand the cards.</p>

<p>The next page, <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/">Endpoints Config Types</a>,
	shows how a test declares which of these machines it runs on: a single endpoint, a set of them, or a fan-out
	that runs the test once per selected machine.</p>
